# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.so.*
*.dylib
bin/drift-bin*

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Output of the go coverage tool
*.out
*.cover

# Dependency directories
vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store

# Node (for web frontend)
node_modules/
build/

# Vite's output, which web/web.go embeds. The files are not committed, but the
# PLACEHOLDER is: //go:embed is a compile error when its directory is missing,
# so on a fresh clone `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...` and `go test ./...` would
# all fail. Note the pattern excludes the directory's *contents* rather than the
# directory, because git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is
# excluded — `dist/` here would silently make the placeholder uncommittable.
web/dist/*
!web/dist/PLACEHOLDER

# Where Vite wrote before it moved to web/dist. Ignored so that a stale local
# build cannot be mistaken for the current one or shipped by accident.
frontend-react/dist/

# The marketing site's build. site/site.go embeds the result and the binary
# serves it, so the files themselves are not committed — a committed copy would
# only be a second, staler answer to what `make site` produces.
#
# PLACEHOLDER is committed for the reason web/dist/PLACEHOLDER is, and the
# pattern excludes the directory's *contents* for the same reason too: git cannot
# re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, so `site/dist/` here
# would silently make the placeholder uncommittable.
site/dist/*
!site/dist/PLACEHOLDER

# Output of the release-binaries workflow.
/dist/

# tsc's output for the AI sidecar. It has its own package.json and its own
# build, so /dist/ above — which is anchored to the repo root — never covered it.
ai/dist/

# Binaries built at the repo root. `make build` writes ./drift.
/drift
/drift-*
/driftwood
/driftwood-*

# Editor and migration debris. Four of these were tracked
# (cmd/drift/main.go.bak, internal/server/server.go.bak, and
# internal/storage/storage_test.go.bak{,2}), and untracked copies kept reaching
# the npm tarball — the package ships whole directories, so anything sitting in
# one is published, and npm's `files` array overrides .npmignore.
#
# Still true of the directories package.json's `files` names, now that it names
# them with `!**/node_modules` rather than shipping them raw: the exclusions are
# a short list of prefixes, not a filter, so a stray file inside cmd/ or site/
# is published whatever its extension. Ignoring it here is what keeps it off the
# tarball, since `files` overrides this file and not the other way round.
*.bak
*.bak[0-9]
*.backup
*.corrupt-backup
*.orig
*.rej

# Environment
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Logs
*.log
logs/

# Temporary files
tmp/
temp/

# npm pack output.
*.tgz
